--_000_4414AEBBCFB2B648B49E5766D309768E98037C9D46MAIL_
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="us-ascii"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable

All,

You have previously received the attached article showing test results that=
 indicate PE fusions do not have the same strength/life capability of the p=
ipe from which it is made.  This work also indicates that higher capability=
 4710 resins don't offer any improvement in fusion joints.

I talked to the author of this article.  He has just returned from an ASME =
Code conference on the subject.  The usual cast of PE people were present.

At this conference EPRI (Electric Power Research Institute) presented resul=
ts of work commissioned to reproduce his work reported in the attached arti=
cle.  The EPRI report confirmed his results.  He said the implication of th=
ese results are that PE joints will not last as long as the pipe under serv=
ice conditions and can be expected to fail earlier than expected.  He went =
on to say that stress concentrations inherent in fittings are likely to cau=
se fittings to fail earlier than pipe.  The failure mechanism is slow crack=
 growth.

He went on to say that the PE industry sent a delegation to visit the guru =
of PE now retired, Norman Brown.  They were looking for Brown to explain aw=
ay the results of this work.  Instead Brown advised them that this looked l=
ike a real issue.

The joint failures being experienced in DR17 and DR15.5 golf systems are ex=
plained by this finding.  Mismatched DR fusions accelerate the effect becau=
se of the stress concentration.  Now we are hearing of failures of fusions =
in line that are matching DR's in these systems.  So what can we say about =
DR13.5 systems?  They were not installed until 2009.  They appear to have m=
ore capability than thinner systems but it is certain their fusions will fa=
il before the pipe.  There are no current means to predict when failures wi=
ll commence.

The foundation of the PE system design method depends on the presumption th=
at the joints are "stronger" than the pipe.  Better said, that the system l=
ife is not controlled by the joints.  With this not being so and with no me=
thod to design for the joints PE is really not suitable for use in high dut=
y, long life, and high necessary reliability systems like a golf irrigation=
 system.

The question is, how do we exploit this commercially?  I look forward to yo=
ur suggestions.

Ironically this implies that a PE system life is enhanced with connections =
achieved with compression fittings as opposed to fusions.

J



